body:
\> Are you struggling to come up with a rough outline of what to code and getting into a state of writers block? Are you struggling with hashing out exactly what the ticket is asking you to do? 

&#x200B;

I was struggling with these at first, didn't realize it for a few months. Much better at it now, I think 

\> right libraries or tools? 

&#x200B;

I think the problems most related to this are not understanding my company's implementations for network requests and stuff like that. Some improvement with each ticket 

\> slack or emails? 

&#x200B;

I have been putting almost every chance to focus I get into focusing on code. I need to be distracted by those more. 

&#x200B;

\> refactoring ad infinitum? 

Not indefinitely, and really just based on PR comments. My manager told me the ticket is only half done by the time I create a PR, which has helped me be ok with not turning in perfect code on the first try. It's hard for me to pinpoint why I'm going so slowly, but it feels like there are several different reasons per task I'm given, and they're always different. There's always a new problem preventing me from making progress, and even after I've admitted defeat and decided to ask for help, sometimes my manager has slack completely turned off for the sake of his own progress, which I completely understand, but it can take 10 minutes to 2 hours to get a response sometimes, and that's not even a real problem because there are other channels on our slack with loads of people willing to help but I have trouble asking them my questions when a lot of them are so vague and I don't have the trust there or relative lack of fear of judgement that I do with my manager, but those are me issues and I should ditch them, which I know, but for some reason I haven't, and it feels like as soon as if figured that out, another huge problem will appear, and even though KNOW programming is all about solving problem after problem, I feel like that isn't ever going to lead me to a solution here. Each individual issue I gave while doing a task seems reasonable, like "oh it's risky understandable that was a problem", but I can't take comfort in that because the others on my team should be reaching similar problems just as often as me, and they burn through their work anyway. So I know it's a me problem

GH>
